_id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": []\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "Valine is represented as a convergent network around the two best-supported valine-specific precursor families: a Strecker branch built on isobutyraldehyde + HCN + NH3 via valine aminonitrile, and an alpha-ketoacid branch built on alpha-ketoisovalerate via reductive amination or Bucherer-Bergs chemistry. Direct but low-selectivity literature routes (acetone/NH4CN, heated HCHO/NH3, gamma-radiolysis, and shock chemistry) are kept as terminal reactions rather than forced through unsupported intermediates; this preserves the real bottlenecks identified in the literature, namely production of branched C4 isobutyraldehyde or alpha-ketoisovalerate in useful concentration, while omitting FTT-like mineral routes because direct valine evidence is lacking.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "water", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "N2", "iupac_name": "dinitrogen", "common_name": "nitrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/N2/c1-2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "HCN", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H10N2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanenitrile", "common_name": "valine aminonitrile", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H10N2/c1-4(2)5(6)3-7/h4-5H,6H2,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C6H10N2O2", "iupac_name": "5-(propan-2-yl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "valine hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C3H6O", "iupac_name": "propan-2-one", "common_name": "acetone", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O/c1-3(2)4/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C2H6", "iupac_name": "ethane", "common_name": "ethane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H6/c1-2/h1-2H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "HCO3-", "iupac_name": "hydrogen carbonate", "common_name": "bicarbonate", "inchi": null, "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Electric-discharge generation of HCN and carbonyl precursors", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_006", "mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-350 K bulk; localized plasma much hotter", "pressure": "ambient", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Electric discharge through CH4/N2/NH3/H2O over condensed water under anoxic conditions; low selectivity expected."}, "mechanism": "Plasma fragmentation of CH4, N2, NH3, and H2O forms radicals and small C/N species; recombination yields HCN and aldehydes, with branched C4 carbonyls inferred within the broader carbonyl pool.", "reasoning": "Electric discharge is the best-supported way to co-generate cyanide and aldehydes from very small gases. Miller (1957) established HCN/aldehyde formation as the mechanistic basis of spark amino-acid synthesis, and Ring et al. (1972) later detected valine in CH4-N2-H2O discharge products. The specific inclusion of 2-methylpropanal is partly inferential because branched C4 aldehydes were not cleanly quantified; this is therefore a literature-guided but somewhat speculative precursor-generation step, and its global atmospheric plausibility is challenged by Trail et al. (2011)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Valine Strecker capture from isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Mildly basic cyanide/ammonia-rich pool or atmospheric condensate; anoxic."}, "mechanism": "Isobutyraldehyde reversibly forms an imine/iminium with ammonia, followed by cyanide addition to the electrophilic carbon to give valine aminonitrile.", "reasoning": "This is the explicit valine Strecker capture step: isobutyraldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, then cyanide adds to give the aminonitrile. Miller (1957) established the aldehyde-to-aminonitrile-to-amino-acid mechanism, and the valine review identifies isobutyraldehyde as the valine-specific branched C4 precursor. Ring et al. (1972) support the overall presence of valine in spark products, but the supply of this branched aldehyde remains the main bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Valine aminonitrile hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Prolonged hydration and pH cycling during standing or wet-dry episodes."}, "mechanism": "Sequential nitrile hydration and hydrolysis convert the aminonitrile into the alpha-amino acid.", "reasoning": "Aminonitrile hydrolysis was fast enough to account for amino-acid accumulation on week timescales in Miller's discharge systems (Miller, 1957). This makes valine aminonitrile hydrolysis the best-grounded terminal step of the Strecker branch."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 8, 72 h, NH4HCO3-bearing solution at mineral interfaces."}, "mechanism": "Alpha-ketoisovalerate condenses with ammonia to an imine/iminium-like intermediate, then surface hydrogenation converts the C=N functionality to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) directly converted alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine with H2 and NH3 over Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 at pH 8 and 22 C. No reduction products appeared without catalyst, showing the metal surface is essential under tested conditions. Ni is geochemically easier to motivate than Pd, but the same study showed strong competition from 2-hydroxyisovalerate, so this route is experimentally strong for the final step but does not solve the upstream origin of alpha-ketoisovalerate."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Pd-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Pd/C"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Pd/C", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 8, 72 h, NH4HCO3-bearing solution."}, "mechanism": "Alpha-ketoisovalerate and ammonia form an imine/iminium that is selectively hydrogenated on Pd to valine.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) reported the highest valine selectivity for Pd/C among the tested catalysts. This is therefore the most efficient direct laboratory conversion of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine, although Pd is much less geochemically plausible than Ni-bearing or meteoritic materials."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_009", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["Aletai iron meteorite powder"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Aletai iron meteorite powder", "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH 8, 72 h, NH4HCO3-bearing solution on meteoritic metal surfaces."}, "mechanism": "Alpha-ketoisovalerate forms an imine/iminium with ammonia, followed by Fe-Ni-surface-mediated hydrogenation under H2.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) also showed that ground Aletai iron meteorite catalyzes alpha-ketoisovalerate reductive amination under the same mild H2/NH3 conditions. This route is geochemically attractive because Fe-Ni meteoritic metal is prebiotically easier to justify than Pd, but the product mixture is less valine-selective and strongly partitions carbon into 2-hydroxyisovalerate."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs formation of valine hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_009", "mol_015"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "295 K then 353 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Concentrated cyanide/ammonia/bicarbonate solution; pH about 8.5; weeks-long processing before heating."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition and amination of the alpha-ketoacid generate an aminonitrile/carbamoyl manifold that cyclizes to the hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) demonstrated Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of several alpha-ketoacids to amino acids through cyanide addition and hydantoin formation in bicarbonate/ammonia media. Alpha-ketoisovalerate was not part of their published substrate set, so applying the same logic to valine is mechanistically reasonable but valine-specific and therefore explicitly speculative."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydrolysis of valine hydantoin to valine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_011"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "353 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "Extended hydrolysis after hydantoin formation; the N-carbamoyl stage is collapsed into this step."}, "mechanism": "Hydantoin ring opening and hydrolysis proceed through an N-carbamoyl amino acid en route to free valine.", "reasoning": "Hydantoin hydrolysis to amino acids through an N-carbamoyl intermediate is part of the mechanism documented by Pulletikurti et al. (2022). The N-carbamoylvaline node is collapsed here to stay within the network size limit, but the chemistry is literature-based rather than invented."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Acetone/ammonium-cyanide valine network", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_005", "mol_012"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "NH4CN-rich cyanide solution in a concentrated pool; prolonged standing/polymerization chemistry."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide- and ammonia-driven carbonyl chemistry involving acetone generates branched C5 amino-acid products through an unresolved C-C forming network.", "reasoning": "Friedmann and Miller (1969) could not confirm valine from NH4CN polymerization alone, but did obtain valine when acetone or methyl ethyl ketone was added. The route is therefore experimentally real, yet mechanistically unresolved because simple acetone Strecker chemistry should favor alpha-aminoisobutyric acid rather than valine. This node intentionally represents a direct cyanide/ketone network rather than an unsupported single elementary step."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Thermal formaldehyde-ammonia route to valine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "450-460 K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "Strong heating of concentrated HCHO/NH3 followed by hydrolysis in an anoxic geothermal setting; approximately 185 C in the historical protocol."}, "mechanism": "A complex C1 condensation and N-containing oligomerization network generates bound amino-acid precursors that release valine after hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Fox and Windsor (1970) reported valine among amino acids released after heating formaldehyde with ammonia and then hydrolyzing the products. The chemistry is best treated as a complex condensation network that stores amino-acid precursors in bound form rather than a selective one-step synthesis. Kebukawa et al. (2017) later highlighted contamination/reproducibility problems for valine in related HCHO/NH3 thermal systems, so this branch is evidence-supported but lower confidence."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Ionizing-radiation formaldehyde-ammonia route to valine", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_014"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-320 K", "pressure": "ambient", "catalyst": null, 