se the pathway begins from preformed isobutyraldehyde and HCN and therefore inherits the unresolved problem of generating a branched C4 aldehyde prebiotically. rxn_003 again needs 2 H2O.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\n        \"rxn_003\"\n      ],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Link this module explicitly to an experimentally supported source of isobutyraldehyde\",\n        \"Specify aqueous hydrolysis conditions for the aminonitrile\",\n        \"Correct rxn_003 stoichiometry\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.82\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.82,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.50,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.52,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.88,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.69,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is one of the strongest final-step chemistries in the literature: Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under mild aqueous H2/NH3 conditions is directly demonstrated. However, the pathway is not complete because alpha-ketoisovalerate is never produced in-network, and the network also omits the important competing product 2-hydroxyisovalerate. The stated Hydrothermal environment should be specified more carefully, since the key published conditions were mild aqueous reaction conditions rather than generic high-temperature vent chemistry, and H2 is not explicitly sourced in that environment.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Add an upstream alpha-ketoisovalerate synthesis\",\n        \"Track 2-hydroxyisovalerate as a competing sink\",\n        \"Specify pH, H2 pressure, catalyst loading, and why H2 is available in the hydrothermal setting\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.85\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.86,\n      \"pathway