 the experiment, and no explicit justification for how H2 and meteoritic metal coexist in the stated hydrothermal setting.', 'FeS/Fe(OH)2-mediated reductive amination is a plausible reaction class, but valine from alpha-ketoisovalerate was not directly demonstrated in the cited literature. The chemistry is also very sensitive to mineral freshness, pH near the NH4+/NH3 transition, and ionic strength, none of which are specified here.', 'The Bucherer-Bergs logic is chemically reasonable and literature-supported as a class, but alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine was not directly shown in the 2022 substrate set. The pathway also omits practical features of the published chemistry: concentrated bicarbonate, cyanide, and ammonia, long room-temperature aging followed by heating, and the N-carbamoyl amino-acid stage. rxn_009 should be written with 2 H2O.', 'The endpoint is literature-consistent only at a very coarse level: acetone plus cyanide/ammonia chemistry can yield valine, but the explicit formaldehyde-assisted C1-extension in rxn_010 is speculative. Friedmann and Miller established the product under NH4CN plus ketone conditions, not this mechanistically explicit sequence. rxn_003 again needs 2 H2O.', 'Fox-Windsor-type heated formaldehyde-ammonia chemistry is a real historical valine report, but the network overstates it as a direct one-step synthesis of free valine. The literature indicates harsh heating, complex hydrolysable precursor material, poor mechanistic resolution, and weak modern reproducibility for valine specifically.', 'Direct valine formation in reduced-gas shock experiments is historically supported, so the target claim is not invented. The main weaknesses are mechanistic opacity, the strongly reducing CH4/C2H6/NH3 starting composition, and the loose Surface label for what is really a transient shock-and-quench environment.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Valine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Valine is organized around the two immediate precursor families singled out by the literature: 2-methylpropanal for Strecker chemistry and 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid for alpha-ketoacid amination. Surface discharge chemistry provides formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, and HCN; hydrothermal H2-rich mixing zones are used for speculative branched-skeleton assembly and for the experimentally demonstrated reductive amination step; concentrated surface cyanide pools supply overall Strecker and Bucherer-Bergs finishing. The 10 encoded pathways are one full Strecker branch plus a 3x3 matrix obtained by combining three explicit alpha-ketoisovalerate-source hypotheses with three valine-forming finishing chemistries; product-level shock and one-pot HCHO/NH3 routes are used only as motivation for the carbonyl-growth modules because the literature treats them as mechanistically opaque.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": nul