,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H11NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"valine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Electric-discharge oxidation of methane to formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K (bulk), discharge channel much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"electrical discharge or intense UV over a reduced local gas phase above water\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Plasma-radical oxidation and partial dehydrogenation of methane to a C1 carbonyl pool.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Formaldehyde is a standard discharge product from reduced C/H/O mixtures. Miller established aldehyde and HCN generation as the basis of spark amino-acid chemistry, and Ring et al. later showed that such discharge systems reach valine after downstream reactions. The main caveat is environmental: strongly reducing CH4-rich conditions are easier to justify locally or transiently than as the global Hadean atmosphere (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972; Trail et al., 2011).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Carbonyl-pool growth from formaldehyde to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"overall-net carbonyl growth in concentrated formaldehyde-rich pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net C-C coupling and reduction within a formaldehyde-derived carbonyl network to generate a C2 aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step is speculative at the elementary level. Heated or irradiated HCHO-rich systems do generate higher-carbon organic precursors, but the valine literature does not isolate a clean formaldehyde-to-acetaldehyde sequence. It is included here as a compact bookkeeping node connecting experimentally observed C1-rich carbonyl chemistry to the C2 aldehyde pool needed upstream of valine (Fox & Windsor, 1970; Kebukawa et al., 2017; Kebukawa, 2022; Ishikawa et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal homologation of acetaldehyde and formaldehyde to isobutyraldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cool H2-rich subseafloor mixing zone; overall-net of two aldol/dehydration/hydrogenation events\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential formaldehyde addition to aldehyde-derived enolates followed by dehydration and hydrogenation to the branched C4 aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is an explicitly speculative bottleneck step. The literature makes clear that isobutyraldehyde is the critical Strecker precursor for valine, but the provided valine papers do not supply a directly demonstrated prebiotic synthesis of that branched aldehyde. The H2-supported homologation logic is chemically motivated by the broader reductive carbonyl chemistry discussed in modern H2-driven prebiotic networks, but the exact conversion remains open (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972; Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Electric-discharge synthesis of hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K (bulk), discharge channel much hotter\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"false\",\n        \"other\": \"spark discharge in a reduced local plume or impact-generated atmosphere\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Radical dehydrogenation of methane with nitrogen transfer from ammonia to HCN.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"HCN formation is one of the core mechanistic results of classical spark-discharge chemistry. Miller showed that HCN and aldehydes feed aminonitrile chemistry, and Ring et al. identified valine in the downstream amino-acid suite. As with other Miller-type steps, the geochemical caveat is that strongly reducing NH3-rich surface gas mixtures are easier to defend locally than globally (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972; Trail et al., 2011).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Overall Strecker-hydrolysis conversion of isobutyraldehyde to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NH3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-rich pool followed by aqueous hydrolysis over days to weeks; aminonitrile intermediate omitted from the node\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-assisted imine formation, cyanide addition to the aminonitrile, and nitrile hydrolysis to the amino acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the classical valine Strecker route written as its overall net stoichiometry. Miller established the discharge-to-aminonitrile-to-amino-acid logic and showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis is fast enough on week timescales; Ring et al. directly detected valine in spark products consistent with this family. The hard part is not the Strecker finish but obtaining enough isobutyraldehyde (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxylative dimerization of acetaldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"CO2-rich H2-fed porewater; mineral assistance plausible but unspecified\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Speculative carboxylative coupling of two C2 aldehydes with redox adjustment to the branched C5 alpha-ketoacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Kaur et al. (2024) make the alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine step experimentally strong but explicitly leave alpha-ketoisovalerate origin unresolved. This compact hydrothermal C2+C2+CO2 hypothesis is included as one possible upstream solution; no direct experiment in the provided valine literature demonstrates it, so it is flagged as a bottleneck proposal rather than an established step (Huber & Wachtershauser, 2003; Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Carboxylation of acetaldehyde to pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"anoxic CO2-rich porewater; net carboxylation node\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Speculative aldehyde carboxylation and oxidation-state reorganization to an alpha-ketoacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Pyruvate is a chemically sensible branch point for branched-chain ketoacid chemistry, but the provided valine papers do not demonstrate acetaldehyde carboxylation to pyruvate. This node is included to expose the precursor gap, not to conceal it (Huber & Wachtershauser, 2003; Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetolactate-like condensation of pyruvate to alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-380K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"H2-fed subseafloor pore network; overall-net acetolactate-like step\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Speculative pyruvate dimerization/decarboxylation to a branched C5 alpha-ketoacid, compressed as an overall net step.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. The missing nonenzymatic pyruvate-to-branched-ketoacid step is one of the main open problems identified in the supplied review. This reaction is therefore a chemically motivated acetolactate-like shortcut, not a directly demonstrated transformation from the provided valine papers (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NH3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-460K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"hot evaporitic or geothermal pool; overall-net condensation and rearrangement node\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formose-like growth of a C1/C2 carbonyl pool to a branched alpha-ketoacid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Speculative. Heated and irradiated HCHO/NH3 systems clearly climb to higher complexity, but they do not directly trace alpha-ketoisovalerate, and modern work does not securely reproduce valine as a robust free product under those conditions. This node therefore re-expresses that poorly resolved carbonyl-growth chemistry as a precursor-building route rather than as a black-box direct valine step (Fox & Windsor, 1970; Kebukawa et al., 2017; Kebukawa, 2022; Ishikawa et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-315K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cool serpentinization-fed mixing zone, pH about 8, NH4HCO3 present, about 72 h\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine or iminium formation from the alpha-ketoacid and ammonia followed by surface hydrogenation.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly demonstrated. Kaur et al. (2024) converted alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under mild aqueous H2 with Ni/SiO2-Al2O3. This is one of the best-supported valine-forming final steps in the literature, although Ni gives substantial competing 2-hydroxyisovalerate (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Aletai meteoritic Fe-Ni metal\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295-315K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar H2\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Aletai meteoritic Fe-Ni metal\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"cool serpentinization-fed mixing zone, pH about 8, NH4HCO3 present, about 72 h\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-derived imine formation followed by hydrogenation on a meteoritic metal surface.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly demonstrated. Kaur et al. (2024) showed that ground Aletai iron meteorite catalyzes alpha-ketoisovalerate reduction to valine. This variant is more geochemically naturalistic than Pd/C, but valine competes strongly with 2-hydroxyisovalerate (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NH4Cl or equivalent ammonia source\", \"bicarbonate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295K then 353K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"pH about 8.5, cyanide plus bicarbonate plus ammonia source, about 21 d at room temperature then about 14 d heating; hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates omitted from the node\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide addition to the alpha-ketoacid, hydantoin formation, N-carbamoyl hydrolysis, and net release of the added one-carbon equivalent as CO2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Mechanistically plausible but still valine-specific speculation. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) established Bucherer-Bergs conversion of several alpha-ketoacids to amino acids through hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates, but they did not directly report alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine. The need for concentrated cyanide, ammonia source, and bicarbonate, plus long processing times, remains a major prebiotic caveat (Pulletikurti et al., 2022).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface-discharge Strecker route\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface discharge produces formaldehyde and HCN; formaldehyde is grown to acetaldehyde, a cool H2-rich hydrothermal homologation gives isobutyraldehyde, and a surface overall Strecker conversion furnishes valine. The Strecker finish is strong, while branched aldehyde generation remains the bottleneck.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal acetaldehyde dimerization plus Ni finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"Surface carbonyl generation feeds a speculative hydrothermal direct C2+C2+CO2 route to alpha-ketoisovalerate, followed by the experimentally demonstrated Ni-catalyzed reductive amination step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal acetaldehyde dimerization plus meteoritic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative direct hydrothermal source of alpha-ketoisovalerate as in P2 is coupled to meteoritic Fe-Ni reductive amination rather than Ni/SiO2-Al2O3.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Direct hydrothermal acetaldehyde dimerization plus Bucherer-Bergs finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative hydrothermal direct source of alpha-ketoisovalerate is followed by HCN generation and a surface Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion to valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate branch plus Ni finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetaldehyde is speculatively carboxylated to pyruvate, two pyruvates are combined in an acetolactate-like hydrothermal step to alpha-ketoisovalerate, and Ni-catalyzed reductive amination gives valine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate branch plus meteoritic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative pyruvate-to-alpha-ketoisovalerate branch as in P5 is terminated by meteoritic Fe-Ni reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyruvate branch plus Bucherer-Bergs finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative pyruvate-based hydrothermal source of alpha-ketoisovalerate is combined with surface HCN production and Bucherer-Bergs-type finishing.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly plus Ni finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"A formaldehyde-rich surface carbonyl pool is speculatively assembled into alpha-ketoisovalerate and then converted to valine by the experimentally supported Ni-catalyzed reductive amination step.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly plus meteoritic finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"The same speculative surface carbonyl-assembly source of alpha-ketoisovalerate as in P8 is finished by meteoritic Fe-Ni reductive amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly plus Bucherer-Bergs finishing\",\n      \"description\": \"A speculative surface formaldehyde/acetaldehyde route to alpha-ketoisovalerate is paired with HCN production and Bucherer-Bergs-type finishing in a concentrated cyanide/bicarbonate pool.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_011\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Valine", "overall_strategy": "Valine is organized around the two immediate precursor families singled out by the literature: 2-methylpropanal for Strecker chemistry and 3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid for alpha-ketoacid amination. Surface discharge chemistry provides formaldehyde, acetaldehyde, and HCN; hydrothermal H2-rich mixing zones are used for speculative branched-skeleton assembly and for the experimentally demonstrated reductive amination step; concentrated surface cyanide pools supply overall Strecker and Bucherer-Bergs finishing. The 10 encoded pathways are one full Strecker branch plus a 3x3 matrix obtained by combining three explicit alpha-ketoisovalerate-source hypotheses with three valine-forming finishing chemistries; product-level shock and one-pot HCHO/NH3 routes are used only as motivation for the carbonyl-growth modules because the literature treats them as mechanistically opaque.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH4", "iupac_name": "methane", "common_name": "methane", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH4/h1H4", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C2H4O", "iupac_name": "ethanal", "common_name": "acetaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "formonitrile", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C4H8O", "iupac_name": "2-methylpropanal", "common_name": "isobutyraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C4H8O/c1-4(2)3-5/h3-4H,1-2H3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvic acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C5H8O3", "iupac_name": "3-methyl-2-oxobutanoic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoisovaleric acid", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H8O3/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3H,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H11NO2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-methylbutanoic acid", "common_name": "valine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H11NO2/c1-3(2)4(6)5(7)8/h3-4H,6H2,1-2H3,(H,7,8)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": null}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Electric-discharge oxidation of methane to formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K (bulk), discharge channel much hotter", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "electrical discharge or intense UV over a reduced local gas phase above water"}, "mechanism": "Plasma-radical oxidation and partial dehydrogenation of methane to a C1 carbonyl pool.", "reasoning": "Formaldehyde is a standard discharge product from reduced C/H/O mixtures. Miller established aldehyde and HCN generation as the basis of spark amino-acid chemistry, and Ring et al. later showed that such discharge systems reach valine after downstream reactions. The main caveat is environmental: strongly reducing CH4-rich conditions are easier to justify locally or transiently than as the global Hadean atmosphere (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972; Trail et al., 2011)."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Carbonyl-pool growth from formaldehyde to acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "overall-net carbonyl growth in concentrated formaldehyde-rich pools"}, "mechanism": "Net C-C coupling and reduction within a formaldehyde-derived carbonyl network to generate a C2 aldehyde.", "reasoning": "This step is speculative at the elementary level. Heated or irradiated HCHO-rich systems do generate higher-carbon organic precursors, but the valine literature does not isolate a clean formaldehyde-to-acetaldehyde sequence. It is included here as a compact bookkeeping node connecting experimentally observed C1-rich carbonyl chemistry to the C2 aldehyde pool needed upstream of valine (Fox & Windsor, 1970; Kebukawa et al., 2017; Kebukawa, 2022; Ishikawa et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hydrothermal homologation of acetaldehyde and formaldehyde to isobutyraldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "330-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "cool H2-rich subseafloor mixing zone; overall-net of two aldol/dehydration/hydrogenation events"}, "mechanism": "Sequential formaldehyde addition to aldehyde-derived enolates followed by dehydration and hydrogenation to the branched C4 aldehyde.", "reasoning": "This is an explicitly speculative bottleneck step. The literature makes clear that isobutyraldehyde is the critical Strecker precursor for valine, but the provided valine papers do not supply a directly demonstrated prebiotic synthesis of that branched aldehyde. The H2-supported homologation logic is chemically motivated by the broader reductive carbonyl chemistry discussed in modern H2-driven prebiotic networks, but the exact conversion remains open (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972; Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Electric-discharge synthesis of hydrogen cyanide", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K (bulk), discharge channel much hotter", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "spark discharge in a reduced local plume or impact-generated atmosphere"}, "mechanism": "Radical dehydrogenation of methane with nitrogen transfer from ammonia to HCN.", "reasoning": "HCN formation is one of the core mechanistic results of classical spark-discharge chemistry. Miller showed that HCN and aldehydes feed aminonitrile chemistry, and Ring et al. identified valine in the downstream amino-acid suite. As with other Miller-type steps, the geochemical caveat is that strongly reducing NH3-rich surface gas mixtures are easier to defend locally than globally (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972; Trail et al., 2011)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Overall Strecker-hydrolysis conversion of isobutyraldehyde to valine", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["NH3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "cyanide-rich pool followed by aqueous hydrolysis over days to weeks; aminonitrile intermediate omitted from the node"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-assisted imine formation, cyanide addition to the aminonitrile, and nitrile hydrolysis to the amino acid.", "reasoning": "This is the classical valine Strecker route written as its overall net stoichiometry. Miller established the discharge-to-aminonitrile-to-amino-acid logic and showed that aminonitrile hydrolysis is fast enough on week timescales; Ring et al. directly detected valine in spark products consistent with this family. The hard part is not the Strecker finish but obtaining enough isobutyraldehyde (Miller, 1957; Ring et al., 1972)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Carboxylative dimerization of acetaldehyde to alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "CO2-rich H2-fed porewater; mineral assistance plausible but unspecified"}, "mechanism": "Speculative carboxylative coupling of two C2 aldehydes with redox adjustment to the branched C5 alpha-ketoacid.", "reasoning": "Speculative. Kaur et al. (2024) make the alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine step experimentally strong but explicitly leave alpha-ketoisovalerate origin unresolved. This compact hydrothermal C2+C2+CO2 hypothesis is included as one possible upstream solution; no direct experiment in the provided valine literature demonstrates it, so it is flagged as a bottleneck proposal rather than an established step (Huber & Wachtershauser, 2003; Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Carboxylation of acetaldehyde to pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "anoxic CO2-rich porewater; net carboxylation node"}, "mechanism": "Speculative aldehyde carboxylation and oxidation-state reorganization to an alpha-ketoacid.", "reasoning": "Speculative. Pyruvate is a chemically sensible branch point for branched-chain ketoacid chemistry, but the provided valine papers do not demonstrate acetaldehyde carboxylation to pyruvate. This node is included to expose the precursor gap, not to conceal it (Huber & Wachtershauser, 2003; Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Acetolactate-like condensation of pyruvate to alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-380K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "H2-fed subseafloor pore network; overall-net acetolactate-like step"}, "mechanism": "Speculative pyruvate dimerization/decarboxylation to a branched C5 alpha-ketoacid, compressed as an overall net step.", "reasoning": "Speculative. The missing nonenzymatic pyruvate-to-branched-ketoacid step is one of the main open problems identified in the supplied review. This reaction is therefore a chemically motivated acetolactate-like shortcut, not a directly demonstrated transformation from the provided valine papers (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["NH3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-460K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "hot evaporitic or geothermal pool; overall-net condensation and rearrangement node"}, "mechanism": "Formose-like growth of a C1/C2 carbonyl pool to a branched alpha-ketoacid.", "reasoning": "Speculative. Heated and irradiated HCHO/NH3 systems clearly climb to higher complexity, but they do not directly trace alpha-ketoisovalerate, and modern work does not securely reproduce valine as a robust free product under those conditions. This node therefore re-expresses that poorly resolved carbonyl-growth chemistry as a precursor-building route rather than as a black-box direct valine step (Fox & Windsor, 1970; Kebukawa et al., 2017; Kebukawa, 2022; Ishikawa et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Nickel-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-315K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "aqueous": "true", "other": "cool serpentinization-fed mixing zone, pH about 8, NH4HCO3 present, about 72 h"}, "mechanism": "Imine or iminium formation from the alpha-ketoacid and ammonia followed by surface hydrogenation.", "reasoning": "Directly demonstrated. Kaur et al. (2024) converted alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine under mild aqueous H2 with Ni/SiO2-Al2O3. This is one of the best-supported valine-forming final steps in the literature, although Ni gives substantial competing 2-hydroxyisovalerate (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Meteoritic Fe-Ni-catalyzed reductive amination of alpha-ketoisovalerate", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Aletai meteoritic Fe-Ni metal"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295-315K", "pressure": "5 bar H2", "catalyst": "Aletai meteoritic Fe-Ni metal", "aqueous": "true", "other": "cool serpentinization-fed mixing zone, pH about 8, NH4HCO3 present, about 72 h"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia-derived imine formation followed by hydrogenation on a meteoritic metal surface.", "reasoning": "Directly demonstrated. Kaur et al. (2024) showed that ground Aletai iron meteorite catalyzes alpha-ketoisovalerate reduction to valine. This variant is more geochemically naturalistic than Pd/C, but valine competes strongly with 2-hydroxyisovalerate (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion of alpha-ketoisovalerate to valine", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_008", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["NH4Cl or equivalent ammonia source", "bicarbonate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295K then 353K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "pH about 8.5, cyanide plus bicarbonate plus ammonia source, about 21 d at room temperature then about 14 d heating; hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates omitted from the node"}, "mechanism": "Cyanide addition to the alpha-ketoacid, hydantoin formation, N-carbamoyl hydrolysis, and net release of the added one-carbon equivalent as CO2.", "reasoning": "Mechanistically plausible but still valine-specific speculation. Pulletikurti et al. (2022) established Bucherer-Bergs conversion of several alpha-ketoacids to amino acids through hydantoin and N-carbamoyl intermediates, but they did not directly report alpha-ketoisovalerate-to-valine. The need for concentrated cyanide, ammonia source, and bicarbonate, plus long processing times, remains a major prebiotic caveat (Pulletikurti et al., 2022)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Surface-discharge Strecker route", "description": "Surface discharge produces formaldehyde and HCN; formaldehyde is grown to acetaldehyde, a cool H2-rich hydrothermal homologation gives isobutyraldehyde, and a surface overall Strecker conversion furnishes valine. The Strecker finish is strong, while branched aldehyde generation remains the bottleneck.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_004", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Direct hydrothermal acetaldehyde dimerization plus Ni finishing", "description": "Surface carbonyl generation feeds a speculative hydrothermal direct C2+C2+CO2 route to alpha-ketoisovalerate, followed by the experimentally demonstrated Ni-catalyzed reductive amination step.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Direct hydrothermal acetaldehyde dimerization plus meteoritic finishing", "description": "The same speculative direct hydrothermal source of alpha-ketoisovalerate as in P2 is coupled to meteoritic Fe-Ni reductive amination rather than Ni/SiO2-Al2O3.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_006", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Direct hydrothermal acetaldehyde dimerization plus Bucherer-Bergs finishing", "description": "A speculative hydrothermal direct source of alpha-ketoisovalerate is followed by HCN generation and a surface Bucherer-Bergs-type conversion to valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_006", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Pyruvate branch plus Ni finishing", "description": "Acetaldehyde is speculatively carboxylated to pyruvate, two pyruvates are combined in an acetolactate-like hydrothermal step to alpha-ketoisovalerate, and Ni-catalyzed reductive amination gives valine.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Pyruvate branch plus meteoritic finishing", "description": "The same speculative pyruvate-to-alpha-ketoisovalerate branch as in P5 is terminated by meteoritic Fe-Ni reductive amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Pyruvate branch plus Bucherer-Bergs finishing", "description": "A speculative pyruvate-based hydrothermal source of alpha-ketoisovalerate is combined with surface HCN production and Bucherer-Bergs-type finishing.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_007", "rxn_008", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011", "mol_008"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly plus Ni finishing", "description": "A formaldehyde-rich surface carbonyl pool is speculatively assembled into alpha-ketoisovalerate and then converted to valine by the experimentally supported Ni-catalyzed reductive amination step.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_009", "rxn_010"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly plus meteoritic finishing", "description": "The same speculative surface carbonyl-assembly source of alpha-ketoisovalerate as in P8 is finished by meteoritic Fe-Ni reductive amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Surface C1/C2 carbonyl assembly plus Bucherer-Bergs finishing", "description": "A speculative surface formaldehyde/acetaldehyde route to alpha-ketoisovalerate is paired with HCN production and Bucherer-Bergs-type finishing in a concentrated cyanide/bicarbonate pool.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004", "rxn_009", "rxn_012"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_011", "mol_008"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_008", "mol_011"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_006", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_002", "rxn_003", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_007", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_002"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_003", "rxn_006", "rxn_007", "rxn_009"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_008", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_004"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_005", "rxn_012"]}, {"molecule_id": "mol_011", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_006", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "outgoing_reactions": ["rxn_010", "rxn_0